{
  "configuration_id": "config_049",
  "reason": "Evidence: Our best result so far is config_007 (multimodal, singleprofile, pca_n2000, imagenet_vit_l, concat) at 0.5353 acc. RNA alone gives 0.4849 and image alone only 0.2266, so the multimodal gain is modest (~5%). We have zero data on whether multiprofile + aggregation improves over singleprofile — this is the largest untested architectural axis. Question: config_049 (multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, concat) directly compares multiprofile with simple average-pool aggregation against our best singleprofile baseline, holding RNA encoder, image encoder, and fusion method identical. Using avgpool (rather than transformer) keeps the change minimal, isolating the 'multiple profiles' effect from the 'complex aggregation' effect. Why this is the best next experiment: if multiprofile+avgpool helps, it validates the profile/aggregation axis and justifies further investment (e.g., transformer aggregation); if it does not, we can rule out that axis and focus remaining budget on RNA encoder choice (scgpt, scvi_n200) or image encoder. This single experiment resolves a major source of uncertainty with minimal confounding changes."
}

